de 3, not Cobalt 2. Record 12 is about H having Indigo 10 to D. \n\nSo unless we can derive that H has Indigo 10 to D, we can't use Record 12 to conclude anything about Ochre 9. How could H get a link to D via Indigo 10?\n\nLooking at the other records, maybe through the chain. Let's see. Suppose there's a link from H to someone via Indigo 10. But we don't have any info on H's links. Unless there's a rule that connects H to the existing links. For example, if H had a link to Job I or any of the jobs in the chain (A, B, C, D) via Indigo 10, but there's no such info given.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through the transitive property in Record 14. Suppose H has Indigo 10 to Job A, and Job A has Amber 1 to B (Record 2). Then by Record 14, H would have Indigo 10 to B. Then since B has Amber 1 to C (Record 3), H would have Indigo 10 to C, and so on up to D. But this requires that H has Indigo 10 to A in the first place. But there's no record stating that H has any Indigo 10 links to start with. \n\nSo without knowing that H has any Indigo 10 links to the chain (A, B, C, D), we can't apply Record 14 to get H to D. Therefore, we can't confirm that H has Indigo 10 to D. \n\nSince Record 12 is a conditional that only applies if H has Indigo 10 to D, and we don't have that information, we can't use it to conclude that H does not have Ochre 9. Also, there's no direct statement about H having Ochre 9 or not. \n\nTherefore, the status of H having Ochre 9 is undetermined because we don't have enough information to confirm or deny it based on the given records.\n</think>\n\n{\"answer\": \"B\"}",
